  "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/usw-pro-max-48",
  "notes": "PoE, read 2026-11-28. https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/usw-pro-max-48 (HTTP 200, Next.js spec table, text layer) prints **Port Layout = 32x 1G RJ45, 16x 2.5G RJ45, 4x 10G SFP+** - exactly the ports this file already carries - and **Max. Power Consumption 100W**, **Power Method (1) Universal input, 100-240V AC, 50/60 Hz (1) USP RPS DC input; Power Input Method AC input; Power Supply AC/DC, internal, 100W**. There is no PoE row on the sheet at all: no \"PoE Interfaces\", no per-port wattage, no \"Power over Ethernet\" budget - the whole PoE section that the PoE siblings print is absent, because this is the non-PoE Pro Max and it is powered from its own supply plus the USP RPS DC backup bus. The PoE member of the family is a separate model with its own budget (usw-pro-max-48-poe.json (720 W)), so the absence is not an omission. The 48 copper ports therefore carry `poeIn: false`: without it `isPoePd()` in `engine/media.js` matched `unifi` in the label and inferred a 12.95 W 802.3af draw on every socket - **621.6 W** over 48 cables, asked of whatever PSE the lab cabled it to, on a box whose own supply is 100 W. Same correction as the non-PoE Switch Pro rows (PR #1712) and `uxg-pro.json` / `udm-pro-max.json` (PR #2111)."
